---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleHero from '../../components/articles/ArticleHero.astro';
import ArticleContent from '../../components/articles/ArticleContent.astro';
import ArticleStats from '../../components/articles/ArticleStats.astro';
import ArticleContactForm from '../../components/articles/ArticleContactForm.astro';
import ArticleTestimonials from '../../components/articles/ArticleTestimonials.astro';
import ArticleMap from '../../components/articles/ArticleMap.astro';
import ArticleFAQ from '../../components/articles/ArticleFAQ.astro';

const title = "Electric Scooter Accident Lawyers Jackson County MI | Logeman & Iafrate";
const description = "Injured in an electric scooter accident in Jackson County? Our experienced Michigan attorneys fight for full compensation. Free consultation. No fee unless we win. Call (734) 994-0200 today.";

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: "Do I need to wear a helmet when riding an electric scooter in Jackson County?",
    answer: "Michigan law does not currently require helmets for electric scooter riders over 18, but wearing one is strongly recommended. Helmets significantly reduce the risk of traumatic brain injuries and can be lifesaving in a crash. However, not wearing a helmet does not automatically prevent you from recovering compensation if you're injured due to someone else's negligence."
  },
  {
    question: "Can I recover compensation if a car hit me while I was riding an electric scooter?",
    answer: "Yes. If a motor vehicle struck you while riding your scooter, you may be able to pursue no-fault PIP benefits through the driver's insurance for medical expenses and wage loss. You can also file a third-party lawsuit against the at-fault driver to recover pain and suffering damages if your injuries meet Michigan's serious impairment of body function threshold. Our attorneys will evaluate all available sources of compensation."
  },
  {
    question: "What if the electric scooter I was riding malfunctioned and caused my accident?",
    answer: "If brake failure, steering defects, or other equipment malfunctions caused your accident, the rental company or manufacturer may be liable. Scooter companies have a duty to maintain their equipment in safe working condition. We investigate whether proper maintenance was performed and whether design or manufacturing defects contributed to your crash. These claims often fall outside Michigan's no-fault system."
  },
  {
    question: "How do I prove who was at fault for my electric scooter accident in Jackson County?",
    answer: "Proving liability requires thorough investigation and evidence gathering. We collect police reports, witness statements, photos and video from the accident scene, scooter GPS and diagnostic data from rental companies, medical records documenting your injuries, and expert analysis when needed. Acting quickly is essential because evidence disappears and memories fade. Contact an experienced attorney as soon as possible after your accident."
  },
  {
    question: "How long do I have to file a claim after an electric scooter accident in Michigan?",
    answer: "Michigan's statute of limitations is generally three years from the date of your accident for personal injury claims. However, certain circumstances can shorten this deadline, and waiting too long puts you at a disadvantage as evidence disappears. No-fault PIP benefits must be applied for within one year of the accident. Consulting an attorney promptly protects your rights and preserves critical evidence."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <ArticleHero />
  <ArticleContent>
    <h2>Jackson County Electric Scooter Accident Attorneys</h2>

    <p>Electric scooters have become a popular mode of transportation throughout Jackson County, but they come with serious risks. Riders often lack experience, rarely wear helmets, and share roads with distracted drivers. The result is a growing number of emergency room visits for broken bones, traumatic brain injuries, and even fatalities.</p>

    <p>At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C., we've spent more than 45 years fighting for injured Michiganders. When a scooter accident changes your life in an instant, our attorneys are prepared to investigate every angle of your case—from rental company liability to driver negligence—and fight for every dollar you deserve.</p>

    <h2>Why Electric Scooter Accidents Happen in Jackson County</h2>

    <p>Most electric scooter injuries occur when riders fall off the scooter or lose control, but collisions with vehicles pose the greatest danger. Common causes include:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Inexperienced riders.</strong> Rentable scooters can be activated in minutes by anyone 18 or older with no training or safety instruction.</li>
      <li><strong>Distracted or negligent drivers.</strong> Motorists texting, speeding, or making unsafe turns often fail to see scooter riders.</li>
      <li><strong>Roadway hazards.</strong> Potholes, cracks, gravel, and uneven pavement can send riders flying.</li>
      <li><strong>Defective equipment.</strong> Brake failures, steering malfunctions, and battery issues can cause sudden crashes.</li>
      <li><strong>Lack of protective gear.</strong> Less than 10% of scooter accident victims wear helmets, leaving riders vulnerable to catastrophic head injuries.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Common Electric Scooter Injuries</h2>

    <p>The combination of speed, instability, and minimal protection makes electric scooter accidents especially dangerous. Injuries we see frequently include:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>Traumatic brain injuries and concussions</li>
      <li>Skull fractures</li>
      <li>Broken bones in arms, legs, wrists, and collarbones</li>
      <li>Spinal cord injuries and herniated discs</li>
      <li>Severe road rash and abrasions</li>
      <li>Internal injuries from blunt force trauma</li>
      <li>Facial fractures and dental injuries</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Even injuries that seem minor at first can have lasting consequences. Brain injuries may not show symptoms for days or weeks, and fractures can require surgery and months of physical therapy. Getting a complete medical evaluation immediately after your accident is critical—not just for your health, but to document your injuries for your legal claim.</p>

    <h2>Who Can Be Held Liable for Your Jackson County Scooter Accident?</h2>

    <p>Determining liability is essential to recovering compensation. Depending on the circumstances of your accident, multiple parties may be responsible:</p>

    <p><strong>Negligent drivers.</strong> If a car, truck, or motorcycle struck you while riding your scooter, the at-fault driver can be held liable for your injuries. We investigate whether the driver was distracted, speeding, failed to yield, or violated other traffic laws.</p>

    <p><strong>Scooter rental companies.</strong> Companies like Bird and Lime have a duty to maintain their equipment in safe working condition. Brake failures, steering defects, or battery malfunctions may make the rental company liable for your injuries.</p>

    <p><strong>Government entities.</strong> <a href="/service-area/jackson-county-mi">Jackson County</a> and local municipalities are responsible for maintaining safe roadways. Dangerous potholes, missing signage, or inadequate lighting can create liability under Michigan's governmental tort liability act.</p>

    <p><strong>Property owners.</strong> If your accident occurred due to a sidewalk defect or other hazardous condition on private property, the owner may face premises liability claims.</p>

    <p><strong>Product manufacturers.</strong> Design or manufacturing defects in the scooter itself can support product liability claims against the manufacturer.</p>

    <p>Our attorneys work with accident reconstruction experts and investigators to identify all liable parties and pursue the full compensation you're owed.</p>

    <h2>Michigan's Complex Insurance Rules for Scooter Accidents</h2>

    <p>Electric scooters occupy a gray area in Michigan law, which creates confusion about insurance coverage. Unlike motorcycles, scooters often don't qualify for no-fault Personal Injury Protection (PIP) benefits from your auto insurance policy. This means you may not have access to the unlimited medical coverage and wage loss benefits typically available after vehicle accidents.</p>

    <p>However, if a motor vehicle struck you while you were riding your scooter, you may be able to pursue no-fault benefits through the driver's insurance. You can also file a third-party lawsuit against the at-fault driver to recover pain and suffering damages if your injuries meet Michigan's serious impairment threshold.</p>

    <p>When rental company negligence or equipment defects caused your accident, you may pursue claims outside the no-fault system entirely. These cases require careful legal analysis to identify all available sources of compensation.</p>

    <h2>Compensation You Can Recover After a Jackson County Scooter Accident</h2>

    <p>The injuries from electric scooter accidents can be life-altering, and you deserve full compensation for all your losses:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medical expenses.</strong> Emergency room treatment, surgery, hospitalization, physical therapy, prescription medications, and all future medical care your injuries require.</li>
      <li><strong>Lost income.</strong> Wages lost during your recovery, plus reduced earning capacity if your injuries prevent you from returning to your previous work.</li>
      <li><strong>Pain and suffering.</strong> Compensation for the physical pain, emotional distress, and diminished quality of life caused by your injuries.</li>
      <li><strong>Permanent disability.</strong> Additional damages when injuries result in lasting impairments that affect your daily life and independence.</li>
      <li><strong>Property damage.</strong> Repair or replacement costs for damaged personal property including phones, clothing, and other items.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>We work with medical experts, economists, and life care planners to calculate the full value of your claim—not just your current expenses, but the long-term costs you'll face for years to come.</p>

    <h2>Why Prompt Action Protects Your Rights</h2>

    <p>Evidence disappears quickly after scooter accidents. Scooter rental companies download and erase GPS data. Witnesses forget details. Roadway conditions change. The longer you wait, the harder it becomes to prove your case.</p>

    <p>Michigan's statute of limitations gives you three years to file most personal injury lawsuits, but waiting that long puts you at a serious disadvantage. Early investigation allows us to preserve critical evidence, identify witnesses, and build the strongest possible case before the defense has time to construct their narrative.</p>

    <h2>Trial-Ready Representation Backed by Real Results</h2>

    <p>Many personal injury firms settle quickly to avoid the work of trial preparation. We take a different approach. Our verdicts and settlements reflect decades of fighting for injured clients throughout Michigan. When insurance companies see our name on a case, they know we're prepared to go the distance.</p>

    <p>That preparation shows in our results—from substantial verdicts to settlements that provide our clients with the resources they need to rebuild their lives. We take cases others won't, and we prepare every case for trial.</p>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground italic mt-8">Past results do not guarantee future outcomes. Each case is decided on its own facts.</p>

    <h2>Get the Legal Help You Need After an Electric Scooter Accident</h2>

    <p>If you've been injured in an electric scooter accident in Jackson County, don't face the insurance companies alone. At Logeman &amp; Iafrate, P.C., we offer <a href="/contact-us/">free consultations</a> and operate on a contingency-fee basis—you pay nothing unless we win your case.</p>

    <p>Our experienced personal injury attorneys will investigate your accident, identify all liable parties, and fight to secure the full compensation you deserve. Call us today at <a href="tel:+17349940200"><strong>(734) 994-0200</strong></a> or fill out our online contact form to schedule your free consultation. Let us fight by your side while you focus on your recovery.</p>

    <p class="text-muted-foreground italic max-w-[900px] mx-auto px-6 pb-8">The information you obtain at this site is not, nor is it intended to be, legal advice. You should consult an attorney for advice regarding your individual situation. We invite you to contact us and welcome your calls, letters and electronic mail. Contacting us does not create an attorney-client relationship. Please do not send any confidential information to us until such time as an attorney-client relationship has been established.</p>
  </ArticleContent>
  <ArticleFAQ items={faqItems} />
  <ArticleStats />
  <ArticleContactForm />
  <ArticleTestimonials />
  <ArticleMap />
</BaseLayout>